/highlighter.plugin.ts
Purpose: Highlights player names by organization color. Who-list/farsight/scan stay configurable regex rules (raw-data, unconditional per-line scan) — but gossip/clan-gossip is NOT a rule anymore: it can't be armed on a command the way sc/score or who can (it's an unprompted broadcast from another player, no local command to gate on), so instead of its own regex it rides shatteredarchive:chat-line (userScriptRuntime.ts's shared chat-probe pass, dispatched once for the whole app regardless of this plugin) + classifyStrictChatSubtype (the same classifier the Chat pane uses) — checking for 'gossip'/'cgossip' — eliminating a redundant chat-shape detection this plugin used to do itself, and as a side effect now covers plain gossip too (the old regex `^[\w']+ clan gossips '.*'$` only ever matched clan gossip). The raw line still needs suppressing before the colorized replacement prints, and shouldOmitLine's checks are pattern-based and run before shatteredarchive:chat-line is even dispatched, so GOSSIP_OMIT_PATTERN is a separate, non-user-editable omit-only regex always included in buildOmitRules, covering both gossip and cgossip shapes broader than the old default rule did.

/highlighter.plugin.test.ts
Purpose: Pins the chat-line-driven gossip/cgossip path (colorizes+writes for both subtypes, writes original unchanged when no known name present, ignores other chat subtypes and non-chat text, does NOT react to gossip-shaped raw-data — chat-line only), the registered gossip omit pattern (present independent of user rules, matches all four real shapes, rejects ordinary text), cleanup removing both listeners; plus a regression pass on the pre-existing who-list next-mode behavior (colorizes after the trigger line, next-mode ends on blank line).

/level-progress.plugin.ts
Purpose: Learns how much exp ONE level costs for the connected character — exp_per_level = (Exp Total + Exp to Level) / (level + 1), from a single `worth` command — saves it per character name in localStorage (shatteredarchive:exp-per-level:v2, bumped from v1: the stored value is now `{expPerLevel, level, className}`, not a bare number) and announces it on shatteredarchive:exp-per-level so the compact HUD EXP gauge is exact from the first packet (levelProgressStore falls back to its high-water estimate when this plugin is off). Deliberately NOT an always-on matcher: no text listener exists until a fetch is in flight — the raw-data subscription is created when `worth` is sent and disposed on capture or a 20s timeout; outside that window it only handles structured events (login, char_data numeric compare, identity updates, connection close). DSL has real reclass/retrain mechanics — NOT the same as the vanilla-ROM merc-mud reference tree, which has neither. Confirmed 2026-09-25 with real numbers, not just mechanic existence: GameLog-DSL_2023-05-0{1,2,8}.txt (the older per-day .txt corpus, not the 2026 Docker *.jsonl one — a first search scoped to only the latter missed this entirely), an example character — level 1 Warrior and level 45→46 Warrior both give exp_per_level=(XP+tnl)/(level+1)=3600, then a reclass to level 25 Ranger the same week gives 1,000,000, a 278x swing from one class-only change, `Reclass@` flipping from "Not Reclassed" to "51" and `Reclass Hours` resetting in the score sheet. So a confirmed value is distrust()ed — dropped, and the one-shot auto-fetch budget handed back — the moment ANY of: a fresh login's level is below the level it was confirmed at; the score-sheet class scan (world-time-and-identity.plugin.ts's shatteredarchive:identity-updated — no GMCP field for class) reports a class differing from the one it was confirmed with (checked whenever a fresh class arrives, not synchronously at login, since that scan itself rearms on login and only refills on the next `score`/`sc`); or a live char_data tnl exceeds the confirmed value outright (this is what catches a same-session retrain with no relogin to hook — a level can't cost more exp than what was confirmed). distrust() is the single trigger point that both clears the stale value and immediately retries, so a SECOND, later contradiction against a freshly-relearned value gets its own attempt rather than being blocked by the first session's used-up budget. A fresh login clears the previous character's class before checking, so it can never be compared against a different character's leftover value. The 'Re-fetch now' action is the explicit override regardless — deliberately NOT gated on canFetch() (2026-09-26): the user may be pressing it specifically to reconcile who they are, so it always sends `worth`, even before identity/level is known; canFetch() still gates the AUTOMATIC background path (tryAutoFetch), just not this manual button. If identity genuinely isn't known the reply can't be attributed to anyone (worth's own text carries no name or level) and finish() just says so once it arrives, rather than refusing to try up front. A second manual action, 'check-score', sends `score` unconditionally (this plugin can't detect whether World Time & Identity is enabled to actually capture the reply) — unlike `worth`, a `score` reply alone can fully resolve an unknown identity, which is why it exists as a companion to the relaxed refetch button. Refuses to store a value unless the division is a whole number. Skips max level and unknown level. `worth` isn't the only source: world-time-and-identity.plugin.ts's score-sheet scan ALSO reads level/XP/XP-To-Level (same command, same moment, so no cross-source staleness) and announces shatteredarchive:score-sheet-exp every time the player runs `score`/`sc` on their own, no `worth` needed — this plugin's listener for it treats a valid reading as a full re-derivation that always wins (not a staleness guess), cancelling a now-redundant in-flight `worth` fetch and replacing even an already-trusted stored value. That listener also SELF-HEALS identity (2026-09-26): if `name` was never established at all (game:character-login missed entirely — confirmed live, GameLog-DSL 2026-09-26, a mid-session character switch while GMCP was disabled means login_data never fires for the new character, ever, even after GMCP is manually re-enabled), it adopts the score-sheet-exp payload's characterName outright instead of rejecting the reading, then proceeds exactly as if that had been the character all along — a genuinely different character's reading (name already known and it doesn't match) is still rejected as before.

/level-progress.plugin.test.ts
Purpose: Pins the parser against a verbatim `worth` capture, the exp-per-level math and its whole-number guard, that NO raw-data listener exists outside the fetch window, once-per-session budget (incl. failed attempts and the new-session reset), 20s timeout give-up, stored-value reuse, the armed-guard against double-firing an in-flight stale refetch, that a RESOLVED stale refetch frees the budget for a later contradiction, login-level-regression and score-sheet-class-change distrust (each dropping the cached value and refetching), a same-named-but-different-character login not carrying over stale class state, that Re-fetch still fires worth before login (no upfront restriction) though an unattributable reply says so rather than storing anything, Check score sending `score` unconditionally regardless of identity/config, both actions declared in configSchema, cleanup reverting the gauge to its estimate, and the shatteredarchive:score-sheet-exp listener (stores+announces a matching-character reading via real example-character numbers, ignores a different character, cancels an in-flight worth fetch with no timeout log following, replaces an already-trusted stored value outright, rejects a non-whole-number reading, and — real numbers, GameLog-DSL 2026-09-26 — self-heals by adopting identity outright when no login ever established one, with a later genuinely-different-character reading still rejected afterward).

/people.plugin.ts
Purpose: Parses player data from game commands into a shared database (peopleDb.ts) for tracking and annotation. The passive who-list/whois scan is armed by the player's own input, not a perpetual per-line scan (same "capture once, rearmed by evidence" family as world-time-and-identity.plugin.ts's score-sheet scan and level-progress.plugin.ts's worth fetch) — onAlias checks a plain `startsWith('who')` prefix (not an exact match like sc/score: who/whoc/whok/whocraft/whois/whoisd/whoisic/whoic/whos/whosi/whov/whoami are all real distinct commands confirmed against the live log corpus, all sharing that prefix), never consuming the command. Deliberately does NOT disarm on the first matching raw-data chunk the way the score-sheet scan does — corpus evidence shows a `who` reply on a populated server arrives as MANY separate single-player raw-data events, not one batched block, so disarm-on-first-match would silently truncate the list to one entry; the 5s window (PEOPLE_ARM_MS) closes only on timeout, restarted (not shortened) by a fresh who-family input.

/people.plugin.test.ts
Purpose: Pins the arming behavior via real corpus who-list lines (GameLog-DSL_2026-09-24, real ANSI-coded clan/kingdom entries): unarmed scan is a no-op, every real who* command variant arms, non-who input does not arm, the command is never consumed (must still reach the server), multiple separate raw-data events within one window are ALL captured (no disarm-on-first-match, unlike score), the window expires on timeout and a later who-input re-arms, a fresh who-input mid-window restarts rather than shortens the timer, cleanup disarms and removes the listener, and existing command-bar behavior (show info) still works alongside the new arming. Uses jest.resetModules() + re-require for peopleDb isolation, matching peopleDb.test.ts's own convention.

/peopleDb.ts
Purpose: Manages persistent storage and retrieval of character data for the game client. Capped at MAX_PEOPLE=3000 (evicts oldest lastSeen down to TRIM_TO=2500) and persist is debounced (400ms trailing-edge) — without both, a `who`/`cinfo`/craft-hall burst calls the synchronous, blocking window.localStorage.setItem once per matched line, re-serializing the entire ever-growing map each time.

